  "debrief": "XNOR is the equality gate: 1 when both inputs match. An XOR with one input held high is a controllable inverter, which is how ALUs subtract (flip every bit) with the same adder. And the floating pin was a real lesson: an unconnected CMOS input can read as 0, 1, or flicker between them, so every unused input gets tied to + or −."
